# No trailing slash, deliberately. `node_modules/` matches a directory only, so
# a *symlink* named node_modules — which is how a git worktree borrows the real
# tree's install — slips past it and is committed by the next `git add -A`.
# NickPurcell/OJ shipped exactly that on 2026-08-16: the blob was an absolute
# path on one machine, so once checked out there the link pointed at itself,
# every `tsc` died with "too many levels of symbolic links", and `npm run build`
# exited 216 printing NOTHING — which meant `npm test` never reached the runner
# while a stale `dist/` went on answering green. Restore the slash and this
# comes back. See Clawcius #60.
node_modules
dist/

# Written by scripts/build-info.mjs immediately before every `tsc`, in all three
# packages. Untracked deliberately: a tracked copy would be rewritten by every
# build, so every build would leave the tree dirty — and a dirty tree is what
# stops `git pull` on the host (ops/src/build.ts), and would also make every
# artefact report itself as built from a dirty tree, forever. Listed one path
# per package rather than as a bare basename, so a fourth package is a visible
# omission instead of a silent inclusion.
src/build-info.ts
status/src/build-info.ts
ops/src/build-info.ts

# Per-instance env files each carry a bot token. The negation keeps the
# template tracked — without it, adding .env.* silently untracks the one file
# here that is meant to be committed.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.log

# SQLite + WAL sidecars
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# OAuth client secrets never belong in the tree; a copy of the one that used
# to sit here lives in the migration backup on the VPS.
client_secret_*.json
